			"106": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "PERLIN\n\nThe Perlin node takes any float type, runs it through a perlin noise algorithm, and outputs a float within a -1 to 1 range. \nThe output value will always be the same with a given input value, there is no adjustable seed.\n\nThe Perlin node is a useful tool for generative images as its output can be replicated infinitely.\nThis allows you to scroll forwards and backwards and jump through the noise hash.\n\nIn the example we feed the Perlin node a Sine Oscillator signal.\nThis signal is \"Perlinfied\" and used for the movement of the Circle.\nAs you might notice, the movement pattern is continuous due to the lack of a seed.\nIn essence the Sine Oscillator is \"scrolling through\" the Perlin Noise hash."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 563.3170166015625,
					"width": 1445.150146484375,
					"x": 93.2999420166015625,
					"y": 1842.4337158203125
