			"24": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "RANDOM\n\nThe first and most obvious node to create randomness is the Random node.\nThe Random node will generate a random float, int or bool upon receiving a trigger.\nThe data type can be selected in the node panel.\n\nIn this example we are using multiple Random nodes, triggered by a Metronome node to create Float, Float2 and Float4 types. These values are used for random positions, rotation and color.\nThe Smooth node is used to smooth between two values.\n\nAssignment: In the empty space to the right of the example, create a small patch that randomly moves a shape over the X-axis without leaving the screen.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 810.34869384765625,
					"width": 1446.0067138671875,
					"x": 92.5,
					"y": 171.5285491943359375
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffff6a00",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Group",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"25": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "PERLIN NOISE\n\nThe Perlin Generator can be used to create a constant stream of smoothed random values.\nThis node is often used to add variation or organic motion to an existing pattern, color or position.\n\nIn this patch we've manually increased the instance count to 20 in the node panel.\nNote that each instance has its own phase offset.\nThis is to make sure that we don't use the same noise multiple times.\nAlso note that when opening the multi-editor (ctrl\/cmd + click the value box) there is an option to randomize all values at the right top corner of the multi-editor.\n\nAssignment: This is a bit of a challenge! In the empty space to the right of this example, create a small patch and create a shape of choice. \nPlace the shapes in either a Circle Pattern or Grid Pattern using the Move node. \nNext, use two Perlin Generator nodes, a Float2 node and the Add node to add some randomization to your pattern."
					}
				},
