			"41": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "SHUFFLE\n\nThe Shuffle node lets you randomize collections with a trigger.\nThis can be really useful to randomize the distribution of colors like in this example patch.\nThe node has a couple of different modes like \"No Repeat\" and \"No Duplicates\" that change the behavior of how the collection is shuffled. "
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 772.96209716796875,
					"width": 1445.150146484375,
					"x": 95.09618377685546875,
					"y": 2486.3779296875
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "fff26eb5",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Group",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"42": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "SEED\n\nLet's talk a little bit about the theory behind randomness.\nRandom values generated by computers are by definition not truly random.\nComputers use algorithms called pseudorandom number generators to generate values that to humans look random. They are often derived from data like the current time and date.\n\nYou might have seen the word \"seed\" pop up on some of the nodes in this tutorial.\nThe seed is a user-defined number that is used to generate the random value.\nIf you change the seed, the results of the random node will always be the same.\n\nFor example: having 10 instances of a Random node with the same seed will all produce the same random value (unless the seed is 0).\n\nBy default the seed is set to 0 which means that each instance has a different randomized seed every time it is triggered. This is the closest you will get to true randomness in Wire."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 773.5362548828125,
					"width": 914.0411376953125,
					"x": 1607.2052001953125,
					"y": 2485.22705078125
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffae7bff",
				"constants": {},
